data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the EF Core Cosmos DB provider **deprecated** synchronous I/O but let you opt in to sync-over-async as a temporary bridge. In .NET 11, **that opt-in is removed entirely**. All synchronous LINQ operations against Cosmos DB now throw an `InvalidOperationException` at runtime.

**Every method in your repository breaks:**

| Sync (breaks) | Async (replacement) |
|---|---|
| `.ToList()` | `.ToListAsync()` |
| `.First(…)` | `.FirstAsync(…)` |
| `.Count()` | `.CountAsync()` |
| `.SaveChanges()` | `.SaveChangesAsync()` |

## Fix

Convert to `async`/`await` throughout:

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

You'll also need to propagate `async` up through your call chain (controllers, services, etc.). There is no workaround — the Cosmos DB provider no longer supports synchronous execution at all.
